**Sticky App Bar Glassmorphism**: "For any sticky, floating, or pinned header/SliverAppBar, avoid using a fully transparent background when content scrolls under it. Implement a dynamic glassmorphism (frosted glass) background that becomes active only when the scroll offset is greater than 0 to prevent text and icon overlaps."

- **Listen to Theme Changes in Custom Widgets**: "When building custom cards, buttons, or section wrappers that read colors from static token classes (e.g. PremiumTokens), always ensure the widget watches the active theme/mood provider (e.g., via `ref.watch`) or uses `Theme.of(context)` to register it as a dependency. Avoid caching these widgets with `const` in parent lists so they correctly rebuild and paint their colors during theme switches."

- **Contrast Safety for Gradient Text**: "Never hardcode light text gradients (e.g. silver or white-to-gray) on background elements that change dynamically. Always define conditional gradients that fall back to high-contrast dark gradients (e.g. charcoal/slate) when the active mood theme is in Light Mode."


Avoid Layout-Stretching Inside Fixed-Height Cards:
When building text details inside fixed-height cards, avoid wrapping inner text elements in Expanded or using MainAxisAlignment.spaceBetween if the text doesn't fill the vertical space. Instead, group the elements, set spacing tightly with small SizedBox heights, and use MainAxisAlignment.center on the parent Column to center the block vertically for a clean, balanced look.

Vibration/Haptics Wrapper Rule:
Never call low-level platform APIs (like Flutter's HapticFeedback) directly. Always utilize a project-specific wrapper class (e.g., AppHapticFeedback) to guarantee global preference checks (on/off toggles) are consistently enforced.

Local-First Caching Rule:
For any user-generated state (like reading history, user stats, bookmarks, or journal posts), always implement a local caching strategy (e.g., SharedPreferences, Hive, or SQLite) first before making network calls. This prevents UI blocking and preserves core features if cloud databases (like Firestore) are disabled, offline, or experience pre-condition failures.

Automated Code Quality Rule:
After modifying files, automatically run flutter analyze and dart format to locate and resolve unused imports, formatting discrepancies, and deprecated members proactively.

